+requires "semantic.k"
+
+module VERIFICATION-BODY-MUTATED
+  imports SEMANTIC
+
+  syntax Pgm ::= "mutatedSolutionPgm" [function]
+  rule mutatedSolutionPgm =>
+    Module(
+      FuncDef("reverse_delete", Params("s", "c"),
+        // Deliberate body mutation: the submitted program initializes with "".
+        Assign(Name("result"), Str("X"))
+        For(Name("ch"), Name("s"),
+          If(Compare(Name("ch"), CmpOp("not in", Name("c"))),
+            Assign(Name("result"), BinOp("+", Name("result"), Name("ch"))),
+            .Stmts))
+        Return(
+          TupleExpr(
+            Name("result"),
+            Compare(
+              Name("result"),
+              CmpOp(
+                "==",
+                Subscript(
+                  Name("result"),
+                  Slice(NoBound, NoBound, UnaryOp("-", Int(1))))))))))
+
+  syntax Val ::= expectedMutatedResult(String, String) [function]
+  rule expectedMutatedResult(S, C)
+    => tupleVal(
+         strVal(deleteChars(S, C)),
+         boolVal(deleteChars(S, C)
+                 ==String reverseString(deleteChars(S, C))))
+endmodule

codex
Static review confirms two independent soundness failures, both with witnesses: the Unicode slice rule reverses encoded bytes (`"😀😀"` is declared non-palindromic), and the task-specific loop bridge can return stale `ch` state (`"OLD"` instead of Python’s `"a"`) because it skips loop-variable binding under its admitted continuation. The same bridge also corrupts the submitted program on `s="😀", c="ð"` by deleting a shared encoding byte. I’m moving to the required fresh false-postcondition mutation now.
Fresh LLVM/Haskell definitions built; all four isolated positive claims close, but concrete semantics diverges on two in-domain Unicode cases. Program-term pinning passes, while the exhaustive rule audit found an answer-bearing loop bridge with a concrete stale-binding witness and byte-oriented Unicode bridges with real-program counterexamples.
